<li role="presentation"><a href='?term="Cogn%20Neurodyn"[jour]' class="usa-link" role="menuitem" data-add-to-search="true">Add to search</a></li>
</ul></nav></section><section class="front-matter"><div class="ameta p font-secondary font-xs">
<hgroup><h1>People see what they want to see: an EEG study</h1></hgroup><div class="cg p">
<a href='https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term="Yu%20H"[Author]' class="usa-link" aria-describedby="id1"><span class="name western">Heeseung Yu</span></a><div hidden="hidden" id="id1">
<h3><span class="name western">Heeseung Yu</span></h3>
<div class="p">
<sup>1</sup>Department of Media and Communication, Sungkyunkwan University, Seoul, South Korea </div>
<div class="p">Find articles by <a href='https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term="Yu%20H"[Author]' class="usa-link"><span class="name western">Heeseung Yu</span></a>
</div>
</div>
<sup>1</sup>, <a href='https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term="Han%20E"[Author]' class="usa-link" aria-describedby="id2"><span class="name western">Eunkyoung Han</span></a><div hidden="hidden" id="id2">
<h3><span class="name western">Eunkyoung Han</span></h3>
<div class="p">
<sup>1</sup>Department of Media and Communication, Sungkyunkwan University, Seoul, South Korea </div>
<div class="p">Find articles by <a href='https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/?term="Han%20E"[Author]' class="usa-link"><span class="name western">Eunkyoung Han</span></a>
</div>
</div>
<sup>1,</sup><sup>✉</sup>
</div>
<ul class="d-buttons inline-list">
<li><button class="d-button" aria-controls="aip_a" aria-expanded="false">Author information</button></li>
<li><button class="d-button" aria-controls="anp_a" aria-expanded="false">Article notes</button></li>
<li><button class="d-button" aria-controls="clp_a" aria-expanded="false">Copyright and License information</button></li>
</ul>
<div class="d-panels font-secondary-light">
<div id="aip_a" class="d-panel p" style="display: none">
<div class="p" id="Aff1">
<sup>1</sup>Department of Media and Communication, Sungkyunkwan University, Seoul, South Korea </div>
<div class="author-notes p"><div class="fn" id="_fncrsp93pmc__">
<sup>✉</sup><p class="display-inline">Corresponding author.</p>
</div></div>
</div>
<div id="anp_a" class="d-panel p" style="display: none"><div class="notes p"><section id="historyarticle-meta1" class="history"><p>Received 2022 Jun 30; Revised 2023 May 1; Accepted 2023 May 31; Issue date 2024 Jun.</p></section></div></div>
<div id="clp_a" class="d-panel p" style="display: none">
<div>© The Author(s), under exclusive licence to Springer Nature B.V. 2023. Springer Nature or its licensor (e.g. a society or other partner) holds exclusive rights to this article under a publishing agreement with the author(s) or other rightsholder(s); author self-archiving of the accepted manuscript version of this article is solely governed by the terms of such publishing agreement and applicable law.</div>
<div class="p"><a href="/about/copyright/" class="usa-link">PMC Copyright notice</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<div>PMCID: PMC11143153  PMID: <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38826667/" class="usa-link">38826667</a>
</div>
</div></section></section><section aria-label="Article content"><section class="body main-article-body"><section class="abstract" id="Abs1"><h2>Abstract</h2>
<p id="Par1">This study explored selective exposure and confirmation bias in the choices participants made about which political videos to watch, and whether their political positions changed after they watched videos that either agreed with or opposed their positions on two controversial issues in South Korea: North Korea policy and social welfare policy. The participants completed questionnaires before and after they watched the videos, were asked to select thumbnails of videos before they watched any, and had their brain wave activity measured through electroencephalogram (EEG) as they watched both types of videos. The participants demonstrated selective exposure as they primarily selected video thumbnails with content that matched their political orientations, and they demonstrated confirmation bias as their questionnaire responses after they watched the videos indicated that their positions had hardened. There were also statistically significant differences in alpha, beta, sensory motor rhythm, low beta, mid beta, and fast alpha activity depending on the political orientation consistency between the participants and the videos. Future studies could expand this line of research beyond college students and beyond Asia, and longitudinal work could also be conducted to determine if the obtained patterns remain constant over time.</p>
<section id="kwd-group1" lang="en" class="kwd-group"><p><strong>Keywords:</strong> Electroencephalogram, Selective exposure, Filter bubble, Confirmation bias, Algorithm</p></section></section><section id="Sec1"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Introduction</h2>
<p id="Par2">Continued advancements in social media have contributed to intensifying user information bias. According to the Pew Research Center (<a href="#CR41" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR41">2020</a>), 43% of US adults report that they access political news through social media, news websites, and the web, exceeding the percentages of those who reported obtaining their news from TV (32%) and radio (8%). According to the <em>Digital News Report</em> (<a href="#CR38" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR38">2021</a>) by the Reuters Journalism Research Institute, 72% of South Koreans reported accessing online news through search engines and news collection sites. The problem with these trends is that most search engines, social media platforms, and news websites use recommendation algorithms that expose users to content that is based on each specific user’s usage history and preferences.</p>
<p id="Par3">With the exponential increase in the amount of information available online, search engines have become important drivers of how people navigate online, and recommendations based on big data and algorithms are increasingly key to how most users explore, select, and consume information (Beam <a href="#CR2" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR2">2014</a>; Cho et al. <a href="#CR8" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR8">2020</a>; Ricci et al. <a href="#CR43" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR43">2011</a>). The influence of search algorithms is increasing on a near-daily basis. Recent studies show that, as of 2021, Google accounts for 70% of all global desktop search traffic (NetMarketShare <a href="#CR37" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR37">2021</a>), and that the results appearing on the first page of Google search results account for 67.6% of all clicks (Zero Limit Web <a href="#CR54" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR54">2021</a>).</p>
<p id="Par4">One consequence of this increased influence of algorithms is that online users can select content according to their political orientation and only view their preferred content, which can lead to the emergence of filter bubbles as a result of selective exposure. A filter bubble is a phenomenon in which users receive information on a subject based on a bias caused by personalization algorithms. Researchers have also called this phenomenon ‘user perspective isolation’ (Pariser <a href="#CR40" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR40">2011</a>), and it has been shown to intensify confirmation bias and political polarization. When people are unknowingly and relentlessly exposed solely to content that is consistent with their beliefs and opinions, their own values are strengthened and they can come to ignore other opinions.</p>
<p id="Par5">This can lead to a polarized social structure in which people with opposing beliefs do not acknowledge each other’s differences and cannot even engage in problem-solving dialogues about social and political problems. Most previous studies on filter bubbles were focused on Google recommendation algorithms and excluded selective exposure by users; the researchers in those studies generally attempted to either verify whether the recommendation algorithms in question provided biased information according to the user’s ideological orientation or simply reveal how the recommendation algorithms worked (Covington et al. <a href="#CR12" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR12">2016</a>; Haim et al. <a href="#CR24" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR24">2018</a>; Nechushtai and Lewis <a href="#CR36" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR36">2019</a>; Sin and Lee <a href="#CR46" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR46">2021</a>).</p>
<p id="Par6">In the present study, we measured users’ brain waves as they watched political videos to scientifically verify the existence of confirmation bias when the political orientations of the users coincide with those of the presented contents. Our research aimed to explore consistencies or inconsistencies between a user’s political orientation and the orientations of the political videos with which users were presented. We also examined whether users selectively watched videos of a specific political orientation based on their own orientation and whether users’ evaluations of videos differed according to their political orientations. Finally, we measured the participants’ political positions before and after they watched the political videos to track any changes in their views.</p>
<p id="Par7">Although there has previously been extensive research in the fields of selective exposure, cognitive dissonance, and confirmation bias, most such studies have been conducted through surveys. Therefore, we believed that it would be meaningful to apply a new methodology that combined neuroscience and surveys to practically verify users’ responses to political videos and expand cognitive structural explanations. The results of this work are expected to highlight the risks of recommendation algorithms as well as suggest the importance of proper political media usage; they may also contribute to existing neuroscience research examining selective exposure.</p></section><section id="Sec2"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Literature review</h2>
<section id="Sec3"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Cognitive dissonance and EEG</h3>
<p id="Par8">Festinger (<a href="#CR19" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR19">1957</a>), who proposed the theory of cognitive dissonance, argued that humans tend to pursue inner consistency as well as consistency between their individual beliefs and actions. As individuals seek to maintain consistency in their social and political attitudes, they may feel psychological discomfort in situations that are inconsistent with their attitudes. These concepts of consistency and inconsistency are respectively described as consonance and dissonance.</p>
<p id="Par9">Psychological discomfort, such as that described above, motivates people to pursue harmony by reducing dissonance (Festinger <a href="#CR19" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR19">1957</a>). For example, people tend to only seek claims that are consistent with their existing beliefs and avoid information that is inconsistent with such beliefs or change their existing attitudes. Cognitive dissonance theory led to the minimal effect theory of mass media by introducing the effects of the intervening variables selective exposure, selective perception, and selective memory, all of which are messaging processes (Severin and Tankard <a href="#CR44" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR44">2004</a>).</p>
<p id="Par10">Recent media communication studies have also highlighted the need for more neuroscientific approaches such as neuro communication. Brain waves are records of electrical current that correlate with brain activity. The types of brain waves vary depending on the activity of each nerve cell, and they exhibit complex forms depending on the waves’ vibrations. Brain waves can be classified into alpha (α), beta (β), theta (θ), delta (δ), and the like according to the ranges of their vibration frequencies.</p>
<p id="Par11">Scholars have conducted EEG research examining cognitive dissonance in a variety of fields including medicine (Wildes et al. <a href="#CR51" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR51">2019</a>), neuroscience (Colosio et al. <a href="#CR11" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR11">2017</a>; Harmon-Jones et al. <a href="#CR25" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR25">2020</a>), psychology (Houwer et al. <a href="#CR13" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR13">2013</a>; Colosio et al. <a href="#CR10" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR10">2015</a>; Ching and Tang <a href="#CR7" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR7">2016</a>), and pedagogy. In one important study, Colosio et al. (<a href="#CR11" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR11">2017</a>) observed that selecting between two similarly evaluated alternatives induces cognitive dissonance that decreases by diffusion (evaluation) after a decision has been made, thus causing the selected item to be evaluated more positively than the non-selected item. That study used electroencephalography to examine the neural mechanisms that were fundamental to post-decision preference changes and overall performance monitoring mechanisms, and it found that decisions related to stronger cognitive dissonance resulted in stronger negative frontocentral evoked responses, similar to error-related negativity.</p>
<p id="Par12">Fischer et al. (<a href="#CR21" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR21">2013</a>) studied the neural mechanism of the effect of selective exposure by examining brain wave reactions to both coherent and inconsistent information. Their main findings were that more theta power (5–8 Hz, 0–0.7 s) was provoked in the consistent condition than in the inconsistent one at the right temporo-parietal electrodes, and that there was an equivalent increase of reawakened theta power at the frontal electrodes. These results support Festinger’s (<a href="#CR19" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR19">1957</a>) assertions regarding cognitive dissonance and selective exposure.</p>
<p id="Par13">Researchers in the political realm have used EEG to identify cognitive dissonance related to fake news. For example, Moravec et al. (<a href="#CR35" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR35">2018</a>) analyzed EEG data for social media users and measured whether they could detect fake news in social media as well as whether the existence of a fake news flag affected their perceptions and judgment. They found that users showed increased cognitive activity with the presence of fake news flags, but that they were also more likely to trust news headlines matching their political perspectives and dismiss headlines that contradicted their opinions and therefore caused cognitive dissonance.</p>
<p id="Par14">As cognitive dissonance occurs when a user is presented with two conflicting pieces of information that they have to reconcile (Festinger <a href="#CR20" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR20">1962</a>; Harmon-Jones and Mills <a href="#CR26" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR26">1999</a>), researchers have conducted EEG studies investigating cognitive dissonance to investigate individuals’ recognition and selection processes. To add to this research area, we measured cognitive dissonance through the brain waves of study participants who watched political video content that was either consistent or inconsistent with their own political orientations. We also studied what political content participants chose to expose themselves to according to their political orientations. Specifically, according to our literature review as well as our study aim, we set the following research question and hypothesis:</p>
<ul class="list" style="list-style-type:disc"><li><p id="Par15"><strong>Research question (RQ)</strong>: How do users' brain waves differ when they watch political videos that are consistent versus inconsistent with their political orientations?</p></li></ul>
<section id="FPar1"><h4 class="pmc_sec_title">Hypothesis 1 (H1)</h4>
<p id="Par16">Users will choose political videos that are consistent with their political orientations.</p></section></section><section id="Sec4"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Algorithms and confirmation bias</h3>
<p id="Par17">As there are now vast amounts of information available in online spaces, the influence of individualized search filters and recommendation algorithms has increased substantially. YouTube uses an algorithm to recommend videos to users on topics related to videos they have previously watched (Djerf-Pierre et al. <a href="#CR15" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR15">2019</a>); according to YouTube’s chief product officer, Neal Mohan, these recommendations drive 70% of the viewing time of YouTube users (Tubefilter <a href="#CR100" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR100">2019</a>). Although recommendation algorithms are convenient for users, they selectively provide information based on the users’ identified interests and preferences, which can lead to information bias. People consuming content that they are exposed to online do not consider it to be content that has been algorithmically selected for them, as they instead consider it to be objective information, and they come to increasingly interact only with people who have similar thoughts on such content. This phenomenon reflects the nature of the filter bubble described above: If the recommendation algorithm only delivers news based on users’ preferences, they will never be exposed to different perspectives, making it difficult for such individuals to broaden their views.</p>
<p id="Par18">Recommendation algorithms can be particularly damaging in the context of political content: Selective consumption of information online that already matches users’ ideologies intensifies conflicts between political groups (Sunstein <a href="#CR49" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR49">2007</a>) by heightening partisan selectivity, whereby users actively accept information that supports their political ideology and avoid other information (Iyengar and McGrady <a href="#CR30" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR30">2007</a>).</p>
<p id="Par19">To reduce cognitive dissonance, users selectively access information that is consistent with their ideology and therefore reinforces their existing attitudes (Sumiala and Tikka <a href="#CR48" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR48">2013</a>); this is called confirmation bias. Confirmation bias is the act of inappropriately reinforcing a hypothesis or belief when it is uncertain, regardless of whether or not it is true (Nickerson <a href="#CR39" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR39">1998</a>); it is also called congeniality bias in that it reveals bias toward information consistent with one’s existing attitudes (Eagly and Chaiken <a href="#CR16" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR16">2005</a>). Confirmation bias arises from the failure to separate one’s beliefs from the cognitive process of judgment (Stanovich and West <a href="#CR47" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR47">2007</a>) and the tendency of individuals to seek confirmation of their existing attitudes. Rather than evaluating the validity of information using logical and rational judgment, such individuals exhibit biased information processing in which information is selected according to personal tastes (Hart et al. <a href="#CR27" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR27">2009</a>). This confirmation bias has significant impacts on a wide range of areas, from politics to science to education (Kappes et al. <a href="#CR31" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR31">2020</a>).</p>
<p id="Par20">The influence of social media has caused confirmation bias to become particularly entrenched within the political realm, thus leading to substantial global political polarization. Recent research has shown that online users indeed tend to promote their preferred posts and form polarized groups around a common belief system (Brugnoli et al. <a href="#CR5" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR5">2019</a>). This is because social media promotes, encourages, and supports global communication; information on social media spreads more quickly and intensively; and content on social media flows directly from producers to consumers without intervention from journalists or experts.</p>
<p id="Par21">Bessi et al. (<a href="#CR3" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR3">2015</a>) studied how science-related news and conspiracy news are consumed on Facebook and how communities are formed. They found that followers of pages dealing with conspiracy news focused more on posts that had been posted on their preferred pages and ignored information from posts on science-related pages that were inconsistent with their own judgments. In a study of Italian and American samples, Quattrociocchi et al. (<a href="#CR42" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR42">2016</a>) found that people used social media platforms such as Facebook to form groups of people with similar thoughts and beliefs so that they could share and spread information from similar perspectives.</p>
<p id="Par22">Based on these findings, we expect that users of political videos will show confirmation bias: They will have more positive attitudes toward videos that match their political orientations than they will toward videos that do not match their political orientations. We also expect that an individual who watches both consistent and inconsistent videos will tend to accept only the videos that match their political orientations and ignore videos that are inconsistent. Based on the literature findings, we have derived the following study hypotheses:</p>
<section id="FPar2"><h4 class="pmc_sec_title">Hypothesis 2 (H2)</h4>
<p id="Par23">Users will have more positive attitudes toward political videos that are consistent with their political orientations than they will toward videos that are inconsistent.</p></section><section id="FPar3"><h4 class="pmc_sec_title">Hypothesis 3 (H3)</h4>
<p id="Par24">Users’ political positions will become firmer after they watch political videos.</p></section></section></section><section id="Sec5"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Method</h2>
<p id="Par25">We conducted this electroencephalogram (EEG) study to determine whether the brain waves of viewers of political videos change with whether the video is consistent or inconsistent with the viewer’s political orientation. Participants also completed pre-and post-experiment questionnaires to measure how they selected and evaluated conservative or progressive political videos that were consistent or inconsistent with their own political orientations. Finally, we observed changes in the respondents’ positions after they had watched all the political videos representing conservative and progressive orientations. We conducted this study with a sample comprising 60 undergraduate and graduate students in their 20 s, and we allocated 15 participants to each of four groups based on political orientation (conservative, progressive) × gender (male, female).</p>
<section id="Sec6"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Experimental procedure</h3>
<p id="Par26">Initially, 150 undergraduate and graduate students in their 20 s submitted an application—which consisted of simple items about gender and political orientation—to participate in the experiment. We then selected 60 participants who could be divided into 15-people groups of four (Conservative/Male, Progressive/Male, Conservative/Female, Progressive/Female) to strictly control the demographic characteristics. To avoid any selection bias, the participants were assigned to each group on a first-come, first-served basis based solely on gender and political orientation.</p>
<p id="Par27">First, the participants completed the pre-experiment questionnaire, which comprised items asking about the respondents’ demographic data, political orientations, and positions on political issues that sharply divided conservatives and progressives. Next, we examined brain wave patterns related to selective exposure to political videos by presenting participants with thumbnails of four political videos on two controversial issues, North Korea policy and welfare, with one video considered to be conservative and one video considered to be progressive for each issue.</p>
<p id="Par28">For the experiment, all participants watched the same four political videos (two conservative positions, two progressive positions), but they selected the order in which they watched the videos according to their preferences. If all participants were to watch the four videos in the same order, an order effect could appear in which the responses would vary depending on the order of the videos despite the questionnaire having the same contents (Herek and Capitanio <a href="#CR28" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR28">1999</a>; Hippler et al. <a href="#CR29" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR29">2012</a>). Therefore, allowing participants to watch the videos in the order they wanted prevented a priming effect (Gaskell et al. <a href="#CR23" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR23">1995</a>) whereby participants could answer differently depending on the context and situation of the question. The participants in the experiment evaluated each video after it ended.</p>
<p id="Par29">The EEG experiment was conducted using a DSI-24 dry EEG headset (Wearable Sensing, San Diego, CA USA) that measured brain waves on 21 channels. When participants arrived at the laboratory, we checked them for EEG suitability by asking whether they had any history of mental health issues, whether they drank excessively, and whether they had received sufficient sleep the night before. The experiment took place in a laboratory environment that had been constructed to block external noise; a computer and monitor for viewing videos had been set up on a desk along with the EEG equipment. Participants were seated at this desk, and sensors were attached to 21 channel points, including the earlobe, to measure the participants’ brain waves. As stated above, each participant watched a total of four political videos during EEG measurement.</p>
<p id="Par30">The final study step was the post-experiment questionnaire, which we used to remeasure the participants’ political positions (conservative or progressive). Figure <a href="#Fig1" class="usa-link">1</a> presents a schematic of the experimental procedure.</p>
<figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="Fig1"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 1.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><a class="tileshop" target="_blank" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&amp;p=PMC3&amp;id=11143153_11571_2023_9982_Fig1_HTML.jpg"><img class="graphic zoom-in" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c155/11143153/6953051bc81d/11571_2023_9982_Fig1_HTML.jpg" loading="lazy" id="MO1" height="405" width="708" alt="Fig. 1"></a></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/Fig1/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>Experimental procedure</p></figcaption></figure></section><section id="Sec7"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Pre- and post-experiment questionnaire</h3>
<p id="Par31">We administered the pre-experiment questionnaire to measure the political orientations of the experiment participants, their political positions before watching the videos, and their selective exposure to political videos. We measured political orientation along a 7-point Likert scale (1 = very progressive, 7 = very conservative) and grouped them as follows: Respondents who scored 1 to 3 points were designated progressive, respondents with scores from 5 to 7 were designated conservative, and respondents who scored 4 points were considered to be moderate and therefore excluded for the purposes of this research. We were left with 60 participants assigned to conservative and progressive groups with 30 participants each.</p>
<p id="Par32">We measured political positions using items rated on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = completely disagree, 7 = strongly agree) relevant to the content of the four political videos presented during EEG measurement  (Table <a href="#Tab1" class="usa-link">1</a> ). As presented in Table <a href="#Tab1" class="usa-link">1</a>, we measured how the respondents assigned political positions to various items using appropriately distributed sentences representing progressive (Items 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 8, 9, and 10) and conservative (Items 6, 7, 11, 12, and 13) that were placed in mixed positions to identify insincere respondents. If a measurement item represented a progressive position, it was coded as 1 = very conservative, 7 = very progressive, and if it represented a conservative position, it was coded as 1 = very progressive, 7 = very conservative. Afterward, the responses to the items representing the conservative positions were reverse-coded as 1 = Conservative, 7 = Progressive so that  (Table <a href="#Tab5" class="usa-link">5</a>) could demonstrate significant differences in the mean of the change in political positions in the pre- and post-questionnaires.</p>
