<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/Tab5/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p"><div class="fn" id="_fn_p75"><p>*<em>p</em> &lt; 0.05, **<em>p</em> &lt; 0.01, ***<em>p</em> &lt; 0.001</p></div></div></section><p id="Par33">Finally, we measured selective exposure by presenting the participants with four preproduced video thumbnails and asking which video they wanted to watch. First, we chose the two issues on which progressives and conservatives in Korean society disagree most: North Korea support policy (agree vs. disagree) and emergency coronavirus welfare policy (universal vs. selective payments). North Korea policy and security are key issues that separate progressives from conservatives in South Korea (Choi <a href="#CR9" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR9">2020</a>), while optimal economic growth and distribution are key issues that divide conservatives and progressives globally (Yun and Rhee <a href="#CR53" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR53">2014</a>). Thumbnails are small images that allow users to see the contents of a video at a glance before watching it, and they directly affect users’ choices of videos. The location, font, color, and length of each video title shown in the thumbnail were all controlled to match each other.</p></section><section id="Sec8"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Video evaluation</h3>
<p id="Par34">Participants watched four political videos while wearing the EEG device, and they evaluated each video in terms of their attitudes, perceived reliability and integrity; the order of the videos was randomly allocated. We measured the attitudes toward and reliability of political videos by reconstructing the items used by MacKenzie and Lutz (<a href="#CR34" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR34">1989</a>), which participants rated using a 7-point Likert scale (1 = not at all appropriate, 7 = clearly appropriate). Five items asked about attitudes and intentions toward the video, including “I like this video” and “I want to recommend this video to an acquaintance.” We measured reliability using five items such as “I can trust this video” and “This video will not be suspicious.” Three items were used to measure integrity (e.g., “Video producers will agree with their words and actions” and “Video producers will surely fulfill their promises”).</p></section></section><section id="Sec9"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Research results</h2>
<section id="Sec10"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Manipulation check</h3>
<p id="Par35">We conducted a preliminary survey before the main experiment to verify the issues we selected for the manipulation (video) and the manipulation check of the measurement items (political position). The participants in the preliminary experiment consisted of 140 undergraduate and graduate students: 94 men (67.1%) and 46 women (32.9%). First, we asked these participants, “What do you think is the most controversial issue between conservatives and progressives in Korea?” The respondents were able to provide multiple answers. As a result, 75 survey respondents (53.6%) stated that North Korea policy was the issue that most fiercely separated conservatives from progressives, followed in order by universal versus selective welfare (62 respondents, 44.3%), incremental versus radical social reform (59, 42.1%), economic growth vs. distribution (51, 36.4%), and freedom versus order (51, 36.4%). Therefore, the issues we selected for manipulation in terms of the political videos were North Korea policy and welfare policy.</p>
<p id="Par36">Second, to select the measurement items for political positions, we asked participants to rate 16 sentences on 5-point Likert scales for whether each item represented a conservative or a progressive position (1 = extremely progressive, 7 = extremely conservative). We confirmed that 13 items clearly indicated particular political positions, after excluding three items that averaged 3 points (moderate). Based on these 13 measurement items, four videos were produced that represented each of conservative and progressive positions on each of two issues.</p>
<p id="Par37">Finally, for the actual experiments, the 60 respondents grouped as conservative or progressive were asked to watch the study videos and determine whether they reflected conservative or progressive political orientations on a 7-point Likert scale (1 = extremely progressive, 7 = extremely conservative). Respondents appropriately identified both the conservative (M = 6.0, SD = 1.10, t = 42.058, <em>p</em> = 0.000) and the progressive (M = 2.5, SD = 1.26, t = 15.635, <em>p</em> = 0.000) videos related to North Korea policy and to welfare policy (conservative: M = 5.5, SD = 1.19, t = 36.032, <em>p</em> = 0.000; progressive: M = 2.5, SD = 1.23, t = 15.465, <em>p</em> = 0.000). Thus, our manipulations of the videos were considered to be effective and successful.</p></section><section id="Sec11"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">EEG</h3>
<p id="Par38">For the actual experiment, we attached the dry sensor EEG headset to seven regions of the frontal lobe: Fp1 (left prefrontal lobe), Fp2 (right prefrontal lobe), Fz (central frontal lobe), F3 (left frontal lobe), F4 (right frontal lobe), F7 (left temporal lobe), and F8 (right temporal lobe) to collect EEG data in 19 channels (Fp1, Fp2, F3, F4, C3, C4, P3, P4, O1, O2, F7, F8, T3, T4, T5, T6, Fz, Cz, and Pz) according to the international 10–20 system. The participants’ obtained EEG signals were stored in the 0.003–150 Hz frequency passband using a 16-bit analog–digital converter at a sample frequency of 300 Hz. The measured EEG raw data were collected using real-time data collection software DSI-Streamer (ver2.3, Wearable Sensing), and the output CSV file was converted for compatibility with a time-series analysis program called Telescan (ver. 3.2, Laxtha, Korea) before the EEG data were analyzed. We analyzed the raw EEG data using fast Fourier transform to extract values for final analysis.</p>
<p id="Par39">The research question in this study was about differences in the brain waves of viewers of political videos depending on whether or not each video was consistent with the viewer’s political orientation. Paired t tests showed statistically significant differences in relative alpha (central frontal lobe), relative beta (right temporal lobe), relative sensory motor rhythm (central frontal lobe), relative low beta (central frontal lobe), relative mid beta (left prefrontal lobe, right temporal lobe), and relative fast alpha (central frontal lobe). The analysis results are presented in Table <a href="#Tab2" class="usa-link">2</a> and Fig. <a href="#Fig2" class="usa-link">2</a>.</p>
