<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/Tab2/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p"><div class="fn" id="_fn_p61"><p>*<em>p</em> &lt; 0.05, **<em>p</em> &lt; 0.01, ***<em>p</em> &lt; 0.001</p></div></div></section><figure class="fig xbox font-sm" id="Fig2"><h4 class="obj_head">Fig. 2.</h4>
<p class="img-box line-height-none margin-x-neg-2 tablet:margin-x-0 text-center"><a class="tileshop" target="_blank" href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/core/lw/2.0/html/tileshop_pmc/tileshop_pmc_inline.html?title=Click%20on%20image%20to%20zoom&amp;p=PMC3&amp;id=11143153_11571_2023_9982_Fig2_HTML.jpg"><img class="graphic zoom-in" src="https://cdn.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/blobs/c155/11143153/b608e58dd628/11571_2023_9982_Fig2_HTML.jpg" loading="lazy" id="MO2" height="998" width="708" alt="Fig. 2"></a></p>
<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="figure/Fig2/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<figcaption><p>EEG analysis result mapping</p></figcaption></figure><p id="Par40">When a participant watched a political video with a political orientation matching their own, there were significant differences seen in a total of six brain waves in seven channels. First, relative alpha (central frontal lobe) was higher when the video did not match the participant’s political orientation (M = 0.0651) than when the video’s orientation was consistent with the participant’s (M = 0.0539; SD = 0.062, t = − 1.963, <em>p</em> = 0.05). Further, relative beta (right temporal lobe) was lower when the video was consistent with the participant’s political orientation (M = 0.3871) than when it was not (M = 0.3979; SD = 0.061, t = − 1.948, <em>p</em> = 0.05).</p>
<p id="Par41">Relative sensory motor rhythm (central frontal lobe) was higher for consistent videos (M = 0.0382; inconsistent: M = 0.0423; SD = 0.022, t = − 2.087, <em>p</em> = 0.039) whereas relative low beta (central frontal lobe) was lower for consistent videos (M = 0.0264; inconsistent: M = 0.0286; SD = 0.012, t = − 1.927, <em>p</em> = 0.05). Relative mid beta showed significant differences in the left prefrontal and right temporal lobes; specifically, it was significantly lower in both lobes for videos that were consistent with the viewer’s political orientation: left prefrontal: consistent, M = 0.0688; inconsistent, M = 0.0727 (SD = 0.020, t = − 2.088, <em>p</em> = 0.039); right temporal: consistent, M = 0.0818; inconsistent, M = 0.0862 (SD = 0.024, t = − 1.995, <em>p</em> = 0.048). Finally, relative fast alpha (central frontal lobe) was lower when the videos were consistent with the viewer’s political orientation (M = 0.0334) than when they were inconsistent (M = 0.0395; SD = 0.033, t = − 1.984, <em>p</em> = 0.05).</p>
<p id="Par42">Summarizing the above results, in brain waves and channels where statistically significant differences appeared, the brain was more active when the participant’s political orientation was inconsistent with the orientation of the video than when the two orientations were consistent, with differences occurring most frequently in the central frontal lobe. We interpret these results as follows: A viewer feels relatively comfortable when a video’s political orientation matches their own, while their brain is activated when the two do not match, because they feels anxious and nervous when receiving complicated information from political videos that conflict with their existing political position.</p></section><section id="Sec12"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Political video selective exposure</h3>
<p id="Par43">H1 predicted that study participants would choose to watch political videos that matched their own political orientations. To test H1, we calculated chi squared based on the differences between the videos the participants chose and the videos we predicted they would choose according to the orientations they reported on the pre-experiment questionnaires. Table <a href="#Tab3" class="usa-link">3</a> presents the findings for the actual and expected video selections (chi squared: <em>p</em> &lt; 0.001). Participants in both the conservative and the progressive groups chose thumbnails of political videos that matched their political orientations, which supports H1. </p>
