<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/Tab3/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p"><div class="fn" id="_fn_p68"><p>*<em>p</em> &lt; 0.05, **<em>p</em> &lt; 0.01, ***<em>p</em> &lt; 0.001</p></div></div></section></section><section id="Sec13"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Evaluations of political videos (attitude, reliability, integrity)</h3>
<p id="Par44">Next, to expand the hypothesis basis for selective exposure in this study, we had participants evaluate the attitude, reliability, and integrity of each political video. H2 predicted that the participants would give higher ratings on all three components to political videos that were consistent with their political orientations than they would to inconsistent videos. In other words, we tested for confirmation bias in the participants’ evaluations of each video; participants rated each video immediately after watching it. We conducted paired t tests to compare the participants’ ratings on the conservative and progressive political videos’ attitude, reliability, and integrity. Table <a href="#Tab4" class="usa-link">4</a> presents the findings for the political videos about South Korea’s policies regarding North Korea and welfare.</p>
