<div class="p text-right font-secondary"><a href="table/Tab4/" class="usa-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Open in a new tab</a></div>
<div class="tw-foot p"><div class="fn" id="_fn_p71"><p>*<em>p</em> &lt; 0.05, **<em>p</em> &lt; 0.01, ***<em>p</em> &lt; 0.001</p></div></div></section><p id="Par45">Table <a href="#Tab4" class="usa-link">4</a> lists the means for the participants’ attitude, reliability, and integrity ratings for all the political videos as measured on 7-point Likert scales; all three evaluation components showed significant differences, and all groups gave higher ratings to political videos that were consistent with their political orientations. This supports H2.</p></section><section id="Sec14"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Changes in political position</h3>
<p id="Par46">Finally, we checked the participants’ positions after they watched the political videos against the political orientations they reported on the pre-experiment questionnaires. H3 predicted that participants’ particular positions would become firmer after having viewed all the videos. Table <a href="#Tab5" class="usa-link">5</a> presents the findings of paired t tests comparing the participants’ political orientations as reported on the post-experiment questionnaires with their pre-experiment responses.</p>
<p id="Par47">The findings in Table <a href="#Tab5" class="usa-link">5</a> reflect the study participants’ responses to the items we devised that represented progressive and conservative positions; the respondents rated all items on 7-point Likert scales (1 = strongly disagree, 7 = strongly agree). We reverse coded the responses to items that represented the conservative position, and the results presented in the table indicate that the conservative group averaged 3.26 points on major issues before they watched the political video and averaged 3.03 afterward; with 1 being the most conservative, the post-experiment average indicates that the group became more conservative after they watched all the videos. This same pattern held for the positions of the progressive group, and these two findings together support H3. All study participants watched political videos that represented both conservative and progressive positions on both issues, and their preexisting views were cemented.</p></section></section><section id="Sec15"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Discussion and conclusion</h2>
<p id="Par48">Most existing EEG studies related to cognitive dissonance have focused on the cognitive and selection process, and no researchers have measured EEG while study participants watched videos. Moreover, most researchers examining cognitive dissonance measured selective exposure and confirmation bias using questionnaires and fragmentary scenarios or images. There has yet to be active brain wave research related to political videos because of a number of challenges: difficulty manipulating political videos, difficulty recruiting participants by political orientation, difficulty securing and analyzing equipment, and the complexity of the experimental procedures.</p>
<p id="Par49">For the present research, we selected two issues that deeply divide conservatives and progressives in South Korea, made a total of four political videos to represent conservative and progressive perspectives on these two issues, exposed the study participants to each video, and measured the participants’ brain waves during this exposure. Because the frontal lobe is related to memory and thinking skills in cognitive activities (Lee et al. <a href="#CR33" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR33">2014</a>), this research focused on frontal lobe channels, which demonstrated cognitive dissonance related to political content. Analyzing the study participants’ responses to the pre- and post-experiment questionnaires revealed both selective exposure and confirmation bias.</p>
<p id="Par50">EEG is generally classified into Delta waves (δ, 0.5 ~ 4 Hz), Theta waves (θ, 4 ~ 8 Hz), Alpha waves (α, 8 ~ 13 Hz), Beta waves (β, 13 ~ 30 Hz), and Gamma waves (γ, 30 Hz ~ 50 Hz) according to the frequency band (Butler <a href="#CR101" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR101">1988</a>; Chartier and Chartier <a href="#CR6" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR6">2003</a>; Klimesch <a href="#CR32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR32">2007</a>; Lee <a href="#CR105" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR105">2011</a>; Ray <a href="#CR102" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR102">1990</a>; Sanei and Chambers <a href="#CR103" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR103">2013</a>; Sayorwan et al. <a href="#CR106" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR106">2012</a>). Beta waves are further classified into SMR waves (12 to 15 Hz, Low beta waves), M-beta waves (15 to 20 Hz, Medium beta waves), and H-beta waves (20 to 30 Hz, High beta waves), and much research has been conducted using this categorization (Egner and Gruzelier <a href="#CR17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR17">2004</a>; Fairclough et al. <a href="#CR18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR18">2005</a>; Sanei and Chambers <a href="#CR103" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR103">2013</a>; Subha et al. <a href="#CR104" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR104">2010</a>). The frequency band that is key to EEG analysis can be divided into the Delta band for waking states related to deep sleep, the Theta band for creative inspiration and excitement, the Alpha band for relaxed states, and Beta bands for active thinking and problem solving (Sanei and Chambers <a href="#CR103" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR103">2013</a>). The brief features of each band are listed in the following.</p>
<p id="Par51">First, Delta waves appear in a state of deep sleep in which the brain is relaxed, and in normal adults, Delta waves that appear upon awakening serve as a basis for judging pathological factors. Delta wave activity is particularly high in the anterior frontal region because it is affected by eye movements, etc. (Kim <a href="#CR107" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR107">2021</a>). Second, the Theta wave generally refers to a state in which the body and consciousness are hazy. Theta waves are associated with physical, emotional, and apologetic activities in a quiet state, while such waves provide ideas for problem solving or generate creative inspiration when appearing in an arousal state (Kim <a href="#CR107" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR107">2021</a>; Hutchison <a href="#CR108" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR108">1986</a>). Third, Alpha waves reflect the brain's relaxation and awakening response; are less affected by clutter;, determine the brain's functional state for human behavior; and are related to the subconscious, stress relief, and concentration improvement (Butler <a href="#CR101" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR101">1988</a>; Cowan and Allen <a href="#CR109" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR109">2000</a>). Slow Alpha waves are also associated with relaxation and resting states whereas fast Alpha waves are associated with resting states. Fourth, Beta waves are related to active thinking and concentration, well recorded in the frontal lobe, and widely seen through the brain when focused; Mid-beta is observed when alert whereas Low-beta is observed when resting. Further, Sensory Motor Rhythm appears mainly in the Sensory Motor cortex, and it is related to concentration as an arousal preparation state (Sanei and Chambers <a href="#CR103" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR103">2013</a>; Subha et al. <a href="#CR104" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR104">2010</a>). Finally, Gamma waves are generated in states of intense stress, such as anxiety and excitement, and they are characterized by being produced during mental concentration (Kim <a href="#CR107" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR107">2021</a>).</p>
<p id="Par52">The frontal lobe is the backbone of attention and memory, and it is responsible for cognition and thinking. This lobe keeps the cerebral cortex in arousal and regulates consciousness to allow an individual to perform a given task (Fuster <a href="#CR110" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR110">1980</a>). Our research results support the results of previous studies measuring the relaxation-arousal response of the frontal lobe, such as the study indicating that people with an active left frontal lobe show more positive emotions when watching movies with pleasant content whereas people with an active right frontal lobe show more negative emotions when watching movies with unpleasant content (Wheeler et al. <a href="#CR111" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR111">1993</a>). Another study similarly showed that the left frontal lobe was activated with pleasant scenes, delicious food, and financial rewards whereas the right frontal lobe was activated with unpleasant scenes, tasteless food, and monetary loss (Sutton and Davidson <a href="#CR112" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR112">1997</a>). The anterior frontal lobe is responsible for the ability to focus attention, reasoning, and emotional control (Goldman-Rakic <a href="#CR113" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR113">1994</a>); related to human cognition, thinking, and creativity (Rocchetta and Milner <a href="#CR114" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR114">1993</a>); and activated when processing new information. The present research focused on the frontal lobe channel because it explored differences in EEG for cognitive dissonance related to political content.</p>
<p id="Par53">For EEG, we used the 24-channel wired/wireless dry EEG headset DSI-24 and attached sensors to 21 channels, including the earlobes of both ears, of the experiment participants. Statistically significant differences were observed in six channels of the frontal lobe (Relative Alpha, Relative Beta, Relative Sensory Motor Rhythm, Relative Low Beta, Relative Mid Beta, Relative Fast Alpha) between the case of watching a video consistent with the participant's political orientation and the case of watching one inconsistent with the participant's political orientation. All brain waves that showed significant differences also showed higher frequencies when the video’s orientation matched the participant’s than they did when the orientations were inconsistent.</p>
<p id="Par54">First, there was a significant difference in the central frontal lobe (Fz) in relative alpha appearing in the region of 8–13 Hz. Alpha waves are mainly detected when a person is awake and mentally active, and alpha has the property of increasing when brain activity decreases; decreases in alpha wave in the measurement area indicate increasing activity in the corresponding cortex. Alpha waves are also associated with active inhibition in information processing (Klimesch et al. <a href="#CR32" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR32">2007</a>). We interpret our findings as indicating that the participants did not actively process and accept information from political videos with orientations that were opposite their own.</p>
<p id="Par55">Relative beta, relative low beta, and relative mid beta showed statistically significant differences in four channels: relative beta in the right temporal lobe (F8), relative low beta in the central frontal lobe (Fz), and relative mid beta in the left prefrontal (Fp1) and right temporal (F8) lobes. Beta waves are increased when performing tasks that require attention (Fairclough et al. <a href="#CR18" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR18">2005</a>) and exhibit high activity in situations that require performance and concentration on physical activity in areas between 13 and 30 Hz. Beta waves can be divided into sensory motor rhythm at 12–15 Hz, mid beta at 15–18 Hz, and high beta at 18–35 Hz, reflecting different brain functional states (Egner and Gruzelier <a href="#CR17" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR17">2004</a>).</p>
<p id="Par56">Relative mid beta (15–20 Hz)—which we analyzed in the present research—is activated when individuals are concentrating on mental activities. The level of thinking seems to have improved, which could be interpreted to mean that the participants were arguing against their existing political orientations when watching political videos that were contrary to their political positions. By contrast, we interpret that beta waves were lower when participants watched videos that matched their political orientations, because the consistency did not trigger any dissonance to concentrate on or resolve.</p>
<p id="Par57">Next, sensory motor rhythm is generated in the intermediate process from alpha to beta waves; it represents the degree of a brain’s awakening, which can be interpreted in the same context as the beta wave findings. Sensory motor rhythm shows high activity when attention is focused and during body movements when external stimuli are presented, such as in cases of simple problem solving, and low activity in stroke patients who show decreased movement (Chartier and Chartier <a href="#CR6" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR6">2003</a>).</p>
<p id="Par58">Finally, to ensure consistency in brain wave experiments, we divided alpha waves into slow (7 to 8 Hz), mid (9 to 11 Hz), and fast (12 to 13 Hz) alpha depending on frequency, and we found a significant difference in fast alpha. Fast alpha occurs when an individual is tense and focused, and it was found to be higher when the political orientation of the participant and the orientation political video did not match. As we saw with the beta wave findings, when the participant’s political orientation was inconsistent with the orientation of the political video, the brain appeared to activate in response to tension from the conflicting viewpoints.</p>
<p id="Par59">We also confirmed selective exposure and confirmation bias in the participants in this study. Before the EEG studies, the participants most often selected thumbnails of videos that agreed with their political positions, and their pre- and post-experiment questionnaire responses indicated that their positions became firmer. Although the study participants watched videos of both conservative and progressive positions on two controversial issues, their existing political positions and values were only strengthened. Moreover, the brain wave activity findings indicated that, when participants watched videos that were contrary to their existing political positions, they showed increased values of more active thinking and focus, but they did not accept the information.</p>
<p id="Par60">The media can heighten viewers’ political selective exposure and confirmation bias. For example, Baumgartner and Morris (<a href="#CR1" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR1">2010</a>) found that individuals had an insignificant degree of exposure to views on political and social issues that were opposite, which is a phenomenon that can be intensified by social media recommendation algorithms. This is because these algorithms recommend content based on viewer usage records, thus creating filter bubbles in which users’ views are reinforced because they are never exposed to opposing perspectives. One method of overcoming the potential confirmation bias from ignoring all opposing opinions is for individuals to be aware of this possibility and therefore aim to expose themselves to balanced information on social media (Garimella et al. <a href="#CR22" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR22">2018</a>).</p>
<p id="Par61">Along with individual efforts, outside interventions and attempts to break filter bubbles are emerging worldwide. The US mobile application (“app”) Read Across the Aisle aggregates articles from about 20 media outlets—including the progressive <em>Huffington Post</em> and the conservative Fox News—and analyzes how much of each source the user reads; if the app detects bias in the user’s choices, it sends a message recommending that the user read articles with opposing arguments.</p>
<p id="Par62">Moreover, the British progressive daily <em>The Guardian</em> explains conservative opinions on specific topics, while the <em>Wall Street Journal</em> shows both progressive and conservative opinions in “Blue Feed, Red Feed.” Academia is also making active efforts to break such filter bubbles. For example, Kim and Lee (<a href="#CR115" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR115">2015</a>) proposed individualizing recommendations based on emotion ontology rather than the existing algorithms, while other researchers revealed how opinions are ignored by existing systems and presented new methods for exposing users to different information (Bozdag et al. <a href="#CR4" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR4">2014</a>). In short, effectively resolving the problem of today’s deepening filter bubbles requires continued, consistent efforts by individuals, companies, content providers, and academia, along with all other stakeholders.</p>
<p id="Par63">This study is the first investigation of viewer brain wave patterns measured while the viewer is watching political videos, and these are the first significant brain wave results in the field of political content cognitive dissonance. To explore which brain waves and channels showed significant differences, we studied a wide range of brain waves and channels. Future researchers should focus on relative alpha, relative fast alpha, relative beta, relative low beta, relative mid beta, and relative sensory motor rhythm, as these showed statistically significant differences in this research, along with the central frontal lobe (Fz), which showed the most frequent differences.</p></section><section id="Sec16"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Limitations and future work</h2>
<p id="Par64">In this study, we scientifically verified selective exposure and confirmation bias among individuals who watched political videos that were either consistent or inconsistent with their political orientations, and we analyzed differences in participants’ brain wave patterns in the context of orientation consistency. In addition to an EEG study, we administered questionnaires before and after the experiment to compare the effects of watching the videos on changes in the participants’ positions, and we had the participants select thumbnails of the videos that they would watch to analyze their preferences. Although we obtained some valuable results, this study has a few limitations.</p>
<p id="Par65">First, the age range of the sample was not evenly distributed; the subjects of this study were undergraduate and graduate students. In recruiting participants for the experiment, we attempted to recruit equal numbers by political orientation and gender as much as possible, but most of the participants were students in their 20 s. The findings for this age group could differ from the findings for a broader overall population; for example, in Korea—where the present study was conducted—the most active users of YouTube are over the age of 50 (WISEAPP <a href="#CR116" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR116">2021</a>). We also conducted the study with a sample of Asians, and it is necessary to consider the political and cultural differences between the East and the West before generalizing these findings.</p>
<p id="Par66">Second, we conducted the study in a region with strong support for the progressive party, which might also have affected the generalizability of the findings. In fact, during the recruitment stage, there were significantly more progressive than conservative participants available. Indeed, on a 7-point Likert scale where 1 = extremely progressive and 7 = extremely conservative, even the conservative participants were closer to the median of 4 (moderate) than they were to 7.</p>
<p id="Par67">Third, our research results support the concept of selective exposure and confirmation bias caused by the Filter Bubble, which was adopted as the theoretical basis of the research. Participants in this experiment all watched political videos representing both conservative and progressive positions, but they only accepted the contents of videos consistent with their political orientation, and they tended to further strengthen their existing political positions. However, these results are only the overall tendency of the difference in mean (before and after watching political videos) of the respondents' political position at the statistical significance level. There were also respondents who withdrew their political position and accepted arguments that did not match their political orientation. This difference can appear through the knowledge level of the recipient (Yu et al. <a href="#CR52" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR52">2023</a>), accuracy and defensive motivation (Bartikowski and Wlsh <a href="#CR117" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR117">2011</a>; Bolsen et al. <a href="#CR118" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR118">2014</a>; Druckman and McGrath <a href="#CR119" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR119">2019</a>; Lundgren and Prislin <a href="#CR120" class="usa-link" aria-describedby="CR120">1998</a>), etc. Therefore, future research could derive meaningful research results by measuring the difference in brain waves after subdividing these groups based on variables that strengthen or weaken their political positions before and after watching political videos.</p>
<p id="Par68">Lastly, the available experimental time was limited. Specifically, because the EEG headset can be uncomfortable when worn over long periods, we designed the experiment to be completed within 20 to 30 min. Therefore, we only presented four videos on two controversial issues as the experimental stimuli.</p>
<p id="Par69">To expand the generalizability of these findings, future researchers could investigate whether the same brain wave patterns hold between Eastern and Western samples, in addition to studying differences in political video usage and evaluation between the two. Longitudinal research could also be conducted to confirm whether the findings we observed hold over time, and instead of merely having a few videos on two controversial issues, more sophisticated research designs could mimic online recommendation algorithms and the content they make available. Researchers could study the findings of which videos viewers choose when they are presented with algorithm recommendations that both agree and conflict with their own political positions, and they could conduct EEG studies of brain wave activity during the video selection itself.</p></section><section id="notes1"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Funding</h2>
<p>No funds, grants, or other support was received.</p></section><section id="notes2"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Data Availability</h2>
<p>Raw Data were generated at Sungkyunkwan University. Derived data supporting the findings of this study are available from the corresponding author on request.</p></section><section id="notes3"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Declarations</h2>
<section id="FPar4"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Conflict of interest</h3>
<p id="Par70">The authors declare that they have no conflict of interest.</p></section><section id="FPar5"><h3 class="pmc_sec_title">Consent to participate</h3>
<p id="Par71">Informed consent was obtained from all individual adult participants included in the study.</p></section></section><section id="fn-group1" class="fn-group"><h2 class="pmc_sec_title">Footnotes</h2>
